imo, an altar is the single most important requirement for a base camp, 
followed by a nearby general store for price-id.

i will usually set up at least a mini-camp at each level with an altar 
(except minetown).  one box with the E word burned into the floor will 
usually suffice.  These will contain spare weapons, armor, and wands, 
and sometimes #dippable potions and blankable scrolls.

i try to set up my main base camp (for spellbooks, artifacts, alchemy 
potions, etc) at the deepest altar i can find before the special levels.  
a coaligned priest is a nice bonus (for the "aura of E-word"), but you 
can hardly count on it, and as Ellora saw, if you have powerful pets it 
can be hard to keep the priest alive.

dungeon branch levels are good places for an early camp, but for an 
ascension they are generally not deep enough to satisfy me.  after all, 
the further you get, the more loot you accumulate.  also, the more 
danger your stuff is in from liches and disenchanters, the more likely 
you are to run out of charges, forget your spells, etc.

having the means to level-teleport makes things a lot nicer.  if you are 
a wizard or plan on wishing for gur rlr bs gncvbpn, the dungeon branch 
strategy seems like a good idea.  gur beo bs sngr is a good one to wish 
for, too, although i usually make do with gryrcbeg pbageby naq fpebyyf 
bs gryrcbeg juvyr pbashfrq (the things are so damn plentiful).

also, i have to wholeheartedly support Ellora's idea of giving your base 
camp that "homey" feel with decorative (and sometimes useful!) statues.  
i also try to keep the walls/doors intact so i can have some privacy if 
i so desire (although, if you summon a lot of monsters, you're likely to 
eventually get a peaceful digger who ruins your beautiful setup.)  i'm 
pretty sure boulders make good surrogate walls, since i think only 
tvnagf will mess with those.

